      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "WHERE",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "WHERE",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "hTTJgBiMqvRUytq_-5CDm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1931.6731432027245,
      "y": 3786.608747356653,
      "width": 924.57958984375,
      "height": 875,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2N",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1026727439,
      "version": 895,
      "versionNonce": 693422735,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1783201633028,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "will use secondary index for this.\nwhile creating Table user can set which col will he use for indexing i.e. where query search\nfor that col so that we make ordered map to track all values for that cols only (save space). \n\nSupports equality, comparison and range queries.\n\nIf WHERE is applied on a non-indexed column, sequential scan: O(n).\n\nDemerits :-\nAdditional RAM overhead due to secondary indexes.\n\nfORMAT :- map<col-value,  unordered_set<pk>>\n\nQUERIES : -\n\n1) WHERE > , < , between (single col)\nfor this my approach is use lower_bound()/upper_bound() - Log n time\nto locate first matching entry, then traverse until condition fails.\nO(logn + k) - similar to trees.\n\n2) WHERE > , < , between (multi-col)\nevaluate each indexed condition. (single col)\nchoose the smallest candidate set\nand verify membership in the\nremaining candidate sets.\n\nTotal:\nO(Σ(log n + k_i) + min(k_i))\n\nFuture:\nUse query statistics/selectivity\nto optimize evaluation order.\n\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "will use secondary index for this.\nwhile creating Table user can set which col will he use for indexing i.e. where query search\nfor that col so that we make ordered map to track all values for that cols only (save space). \n\nSupports equality, comparison and range queries.\n\nIf WHERE is applied on a non-indexed column, sequential scan: O(n).\n\nDemerits :-\nAdditional RAM overhead due to secondary indexes.\n\nfORMAT :- map<col-value,  unordered_set<pk>>\n\nQUERIES : -\n\n1) WHERE > , < , between (single col)\nfor this my approach is use lower_bound()/upper_bound() - Log n time\nto locate first matching entry, then traverse until condition fails.\nO(logn + k) - similar to trees.\n\n2) WHERE > , < , between (multi-col)\nevaluate each indexed condition. (single col)\nchoose the smallest candidate set\nand verify membership in the\nremaining candidate sets.\n\nTotal:\nO(Σ(log n + k_i) + min(k_i))\n\nFuture:\nUse query statistics/selectivity\nto optimize evaluation order.\n\n\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
